  "predictions": [
    {
      "claim": "With the deletion rate at zero the inferred alignment is exactly the input, so no Phase 2 result changes by passing through the aligner.",
      "corrected_after_a_first_run": "A1 failed at 1 of 5 seeds on the first implementation, and the cause was not a bug in the DP: with a -10/-1 affine gap, a run of three mismatches scores exactly what deleting one reference residue and inserting one query residue scores (-12 + 5 against -11 + 15 - 11), so the identity was one of two optimal alignments and the aligner returned the other. The fix was to state the tie preference — M over X over Y, a substitution over a gap pair — which is why the criterion now checks the score as well as the rows. Regression test: tests/aligner_identity.rs.",
      "criterion": "inferred rows == truth rows at every seed, and the optimal score equals the diagonal score",
      "gated": true,
      "id": "A1"
    },
    {
      "claim": "At this deletion load the aligner recovers almost every column.",
      "criterion": "worst-seed column recovery > 0.99",
      "gated": false,
      "id": "A2"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Alignment error manufactures no four-point violation on a genealogy that is exactly a tree.",
      "criterion": "manufactured_by_alignment == 0 at every seed",
      "gated": false,
      "id": "A3"
    }
  ],
  "why": "A Lean certificate proves an arithmetic fact about counts taken from the sequences it embeds. If the alignment placed the wrong residues in the same column, the counts are right and the data is wrong, and the kernel cannot see it. Real data adds the aligner to the trust surface; this measures how big that addition is, on populations with no reticulation in them at all."
